5 using static Andirit.DurableCommandLog;
9 /// <summary>Durable embedded Andirit store backed by LatticeDB.</summary>
12 /// LatticeDB is the durable command log; all graph semantics and query behavior
20 /// Ownership and errors: a database file has a single owner process while open.
21 /// Opening a second store on the same path (in- or out-of-process) fails rather
32 /// Cancellation contract: a cancellation request is honoured while an operation
35 /// the pinned native dependency exposes no cancellation or interruption entry
36 /// point, so a cancellation that arrives during active native execution is not
37 /// observed, the transaction commits atomically, and the operation completes.
38 /// A cancelled operation therefore never exposes partial facts or index
39 /// entries, and a durable mutation is never reported as cancelled.
42 /// Cancellation outcomes are truthful and limited to three: the operation was
43 /// cancelled before native invocation (an
44 /// <see cref="OperationCanceledException"/> is thrown and no native write
45 /// occurred); the operation completed; or the commit outcome is unknown
46 /// (<see cref="CodeGraphCommitOutcomeUnknownException"/>, after which the
47 /// instance refuses current-state claims until the durable state is
48 /// corroborated by reopening the store). A managed timeout, an abandoned task
49 /// or a disposed caller is <b>not</b> a native cancellation and must never be
50 /// reported as one: callers must await the operation to learn its outcome.
53 /// Durability contract: the <see cref="LatticeTransaction.Commit"/> operation is
54 /// the durability point. After a successful commit, the outcome must not be
55 /// negated by a subsequent cancellation or crash; the store already refuses to
